Z||000014||hugh||06-27-2000||06:42 PM||||I just remembered,Dennis Kirban just north of Philly has a switch that is a real good starter kill.You have to touch two points(similar to one of those lamps)in order to activate the starter.You can make them anything you want.Two spots on the dash or wherever.I believe the price was about $55.00.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||06-27-2000||10:55 PM||||My friend and I dreamed up a good one for his 67 Camaro.  We dismantled the Cigarette lighter and rigged it as a Kill switch.  Basically, the cigarette lighter had a piece of metal on the end that completed a circuit behind the dash when you pressed it in.<P>It looked all original, and the car never got stolen.  Of course, he pretty much kept it locked up in his grandfathers shop in the evening, but it never got stolen.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||06-28-2000||04:57 PM||||Acording to what I'm reading in the haynes book, the only line you can cut that will totally kill the fuel pump is the wire to the fuel pump fuse. You should use a switch that is at least the same current rating as the fuse, in this case you want 10amp minimum contacts in the switch.<P>An alternate that keeps the ECM from precharging the system would be to interupt the F.P. relay wires. The problem with this is that if you crank an engine enough it will build the oil pressure enough to close that switch at what point the car will start. The contacts in the oil presure switch and the F. P. relay are wired in parrallel.<P>I wouldn't mess with the plug wires much. They tend not to like allot of pulling, especially when they are warm.<P>If I was doing the ignition killer I'd chop the 12v line to the PIM on the distributor. Not short the coil to ground. Shorting the coil shorts the PIM and dumps allot of current thru the coil driver transistor which could fry it.<P>I would not do an ignition killer on the DIS motor. That PIM and coil pack draws a hell of allot more power than the single coil motors. If a switch added to that system develops any resistance at all then it can severely reduce current to the coils. The switch would also have to be added into the car's rear harness someplace. The DIS brick and the ECM use the same fuse so you can't just do this at the fuse block. (You can damage the ECM if you run it with one of it's power lines disconnected.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||06-28-2000||11:17 PM||||Im not a Sears salesman, but Sears sells a replacement battery that has a keychain remote that disconnects power internally in the battery so it cannot start or run. It still lets enough power thru to keep cmptr memory and radio presets. Ez way to go, a new battery cant never be bad anyway, and dont have to do any special wiring or switching, also can be used if your carjacked too. Remember if someone wants ur car bad enough, they can just tow it off no matter what you do.||||reg||1
